.TH AENIGMA-VPN-SERVER 8 "2026-08-13" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-vpn-server \- initialize a PKI and configure an OpenVPN
server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-vpn-server
.B \-d
.I DOMAIN
.B \-p
.I PORT
.B \-a
.I ADDRESS
.B \-n
.I NETMASK
.br
.B aenigma-vpn-server
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-vpn-server
installs OpenVPN and Easy-RSA, if not already present, initializes a
certificate authority (on first run only), issues a server
certificate, and writes and starts an OpenVPN server configuration.
.PP
The script performs the following actions:
.IP \(bu 2
Installs the
.B openvpn
and
.B easy-rsa
packages via
.BR apt .
.IP \(bu 2
Creates an unprivileged, login-disabled system user
.B openvpn
if it does not already exist.
.IP \(bu 2
Creates the server's working directory
.RI ( /etc/openvpn/ DOMAIN ),
its client-config-dir
.RI ( /etc/openvpn/ccd/ DOMAIN ),
and its log directory
.RI ( /var/log/openvpn/ DOMAIN ).
.IP \(bu 2
.B Only if
.I /etc/openvpn/easy\-rsa/pki/ca.crt
does not already exist: initializes a new Easy-RSA PKI directory,
generates a certificate authority (with no CA private key
passphrase), and generates Diffie-Hellman parameters, copying the
resulting
.I ca.crt
and
.I dh.pem
to
.IR /etc/openvpn .
This step is skipped on subsequent runs, so all domains configured
on the same host with this script share a single certificate
authority.
.IP \(bu 2
.B On every run,
unconditionally: generates a new certificate signing request for
.I DOMAIN
(with no key passphrase) and signs it as a server certificate using
the CA, replacing any previously issued certificate/key for the same
.IR DOMAIN .
.IP \(bu 2
Copies the newly issued certificate and key into the server's
directory, restricting the private key's ownership to
.B openvpn:openvpn
and permissions to
.BR 700 .
.IP \(bu 2
Generates a new TLS authentication static key
.RI ( ta.key )
and applies the same ownership/permission restrictions to it.
.IP \(bu 2
Writes an OpenVPN
.B server
configuration file to
.IR /etc/openvpn/ DOMAIN .conf,
overwriting it in full, and enables and restarts the corresponding
.B openvpn@ DOMAIN
systemd service instance.
.PP
.B aenigma-vpn-server
must be run as root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " DOMAIN"
Domain name (or address) clients will use to reach this server, e.g.
.IR example.com .
Also used as the certificate common name, and as the base name of
the generated server directory, configuration file, and
.B openvpn@
systemd instance. Required.
.TP
.BI \-p " PORT"
UDP port the server should listen on, e.g.
.IR 1194 .
Required.
.TP
.BI \-a " ADDRESS"
VPN network address to serve to clients, e.g.
.IR 10.8.0.0 .
Required.
.TP
.BI \-n " NETMASK"
Netmask for the VPN network, e.g.
.IR 255.255.255.0 .
Required.
.TP
.B \-h
Display a usage summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The server certificate was issued (or reissued) and the
.B openvpn@ DOMAIN
service started successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid or missing arguments, the script was not run as root, or an
.B easy-rsa
or
.B openvpn
command failed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/openvpn/easy\-rsa/pki/
Easy-RSA PKI directory, including the certificate authority's own
key and certificate. Created only if absent; shared across every
domain configured with this script on the same host.
.TP
.I /etc/openvpn/ca.crt ", " /etc/openvpn/dh.pem
Copies of the CA certificate and Diffie-Hellman parameters, shared
across all server domains configured on this host.
.TP
.I /etc/openvpn/ DOMAIN /
Per-domain server directory, containing the issued server
certificate and key, the TLS authentication key, and the
client-IP-pool persistence file.
.TP
.I /etc/openvpn/ DOMAIN .conf
Generated OpenVPN
.B server
configuration file. Overwritten in full on each run.
.TP
.I /etc/openvpn/ccd/ DOMAIN /
Per-client configuration directory for this server, referenced by
.B client-config-dir
in the generated configuration.
.TP
.I /var/log/openvpn/ DOMAIN /
Log and status file directory for this server instance.
.SH EXAMPLES
Initialize the PKI (on first run) and start a VPN server:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo aenigma-vpn-server \-d example.com \-p 1194 \e
    \-a 10.8.0.0 \-n 255.255.255.0
.EE
.RE
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The certificate authority's private key is generated with
.B nopass
(no passphrase) and is not otherwise encrypted at rest. Anyone able
to read
.I /etc/openvpn/easy\-rsa/pki/private/ca.key
can issue arbitrary client or server certificates trusted by every
VPN endpoint using this CA. This directory should be protected
accordingly, and ideally kept on infrastructure separate from
routine client-management operations if a stronger security posture
than this script provides by default is required.
.PP
The server's own private key is likewise generated with no
passphrase; possession of the file alone is sufficient to
impersonate this server to connecting clients.
.SH NOTES
The
.I /etc/openvpn/ DOMAIN .conf
path written by this script is the same naming convention used by
.BR aenigma-vpn-client (8)
for its
.B client
configuration file, and is required by
.BR aenigma-vpn-dns (8)
to already exist as a
.B server
configuration when appending its DHCP-option push directives.
.BR aenigma-vpn-server (8)
and
.BR aenigma-vpn-client (8)
are intended to run on different hosts (the VPN server and a VPN
client, respectively); running both against the same
.I DOMAIN
on the same host will result in whichever script runs last
overwriting the other's configuration at the same path, silently
converting the host from a VPN server into a VPN client or vice
versa.
.PP
Unlike CA and Diffie-Hellman parameter generation, which are guarded
and only performed on first run, the server certificate and key are
regenerated
.B unconditionally
on every invocation of this script for the same
.IR DOMAIN .
Re-running this script issues a new server certificate, discards the
previous one, and restarts the service, interrupting any active VPN
sessions. Client certificates signed against the same CA remain
valid, since only the server's own certificate is replaced.
.PP
Only the server's private key and TLS authentication key have their
ownership and permissions explicitly restricted by this script;
.I ca.crt
and
.I dh.pem
are left with whatever ownership and permissions
.BR easy-rsa (1)
and the subsequent
.B cp
produce, which is appropriate since both are public material, but
worth noting for consistency with the private key handling described
above.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR openvpn (8),
.BR easyrsa (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR aenigma-vpn-client (8),
.BR aenigma-vpn-dns (8)
